  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to find a balanced step function that minimizes maximum overlap with its complement across all shifts. This two-stage approach uses a distributed representation transforming the problem into a constrained optimization with box constraints and equality constraints. The first stage involves collective optimization where each coordinate is represented by multiple pseudo-variables to approximate inputs to correlated subcomponents. The second stage adapts the solution to various resolutions through polynomial basis interpolation, maintaining the integral constraint via a two-parameter family of solutions.\n\nThis approach addresses the non-convex optimization by:\n- Transforming problem for better scaling\n- Using dual-stage optimization\n- Maintaining integral constraint via polynomial bases\n- Adapting to different resolutions\n\nImportant limitations include:\n- Requires careful repair mechanisms\n- Objective non-convexity persists despite transformation\n- Polynomial basis introduces approximation error\n- Repairing integral constraint is non-trivial\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: \n  - Sample rate resolution n_points\n  - Input vector h \u2208 [0,1]^{n_points} with \u2211h_i = n_points/2\n  - n\u25d0-Dimensional coordinates transformed into d-dimensional groups\n  - Step functions represented via polynomial basis p_k(x) = (1 + cos(k \u03c0 x))\n\nObjectives:\n- Minimize: max_k (\u0394\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx) * (dx\u207b\u00b9)\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 \u2200i\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n- Integral preservation via global optimization constraints\n\nDerivatives:\n- Jacobians computed via automatic differentiation\n- Constraint projections via Lagrange multiplier\n- Approximation gradients through BPAD framework\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Parent validation:\n  - Verify sum(h)/2 matches sample count\n  - Compute initial correlation metric\n- Core update loop:\n  - Initial template generation with baseline polynomial forms\n  - Dual-stage exploitation-exploration\n    Primary stage: Parallel coordinate optimization\n    Secondary stage: Resolution adaptation via FFT interpolation\n  - Repair: Integral preservation via projection\n- Objective evaluation:\n  - Full correlation computation\n  - Scaling by dx = 2.0/n_points\n  - Lag space integration\n- Dimensionality management:\n  - Sample rate transformations\n  - FFT-based interpolation\n- Constraint enforcement:\n  - Box-constraint repair via clipping\n  - Sum repair via gradient projection\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Configuration:\n  - n_points distribution: Logger [80,160]\n  - Exchange rate: 0.75 (Type I to Type II exchange)\n  - Exploration amounts: \n    Lagged-gradient: 0.5\n    Harmonic: 0.75\n    Direct: 0.4\n- Back-transition rates:\n  - Gradual: 0.6\n  - Jump: 0.8\n  - Adaptation: 0.7\n- Communication budget:\n  - k1: 0.2*n_points\n  - k2: 0.4*n_points\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Dominant costs:\n  - O(n_points\u00b2) correlation at each evaluation\n  - O(\u03bc) expensive but periodic constraint checks\n- Bounds checking scheduled per:\n  - Lag: Every 5000 evaluations\n  - Resolution: Every 3000 evaluations\n- Budget allocation:\n  - Set: 58%\n  - Verification: 22%\n  - Repair: 20%\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Feasibility checks:\n  - Integral preservation tolerance < 1e-4\n  - Box constraints enforced via clipping\n  - Correlation value properly scaled\n- Interface:\n  - Returns (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n  - Must pass validation:\n    sum(h_values) / n_points * 2 \u2248 1\n  - Post-condition checks on fallback\n- Cost modeling:\n  - O(n_points\u00b2) per evaluation\n  - Bounds: n_points \u2208 [80,160]\n\nConstraints always enforced in repair step.",
